The suite uses the actual reusable mudlib coordinator through coordinator_source.c
and a local wrapper with small compile-time bounds. It never uses a production
mudlib or an external checkout. Standard test/run.sh discovers this directory.

Reports cover exact object_name() fields (run in COMPAT and non-COMPAT modes),
all matching raw/named/Python dependencies, ordinary schema/structural failure,
140 matching instances, shared work limits, isolated nested report copies,
removed declarations on success/failure, and diagnostic allocation/GC roots.

The operator workflow covers deny-default/current policy, separate command and
wizard authority, driver ownership, policy selection mutation/reentry, all and
explicit-empty selections, loaded/path sources, already-current dependencies,
local capacity, poll exhaustion, scheduling failure, local expiry and driver TTL,
historical clones selection, retained state, and no initializer replay.

The report allocation sweep recognizes an ordinary build by an uninjected point
zero, still validates the real report and successful recovery, and reports zero
injected boundaries. Instrumented builds sweep through all diagnostic boundaries.
Python-specific rows are skipped explicitly when Python support is absent.

Successful completion prints BLUEPRINT_WORKFLOW: N cases passed. with N > 0.
A disabled driver prints BLUEPRINT_WORKFLOW: feature disabled. instead.

Private driver clock/work/copy-budget controls are asserted only in instrumented
builds. Ordinary enabled builds still exercise real reports, all coordinator
source/selection modes, policy, local expiry, and successful recovery. Each GC
check removes its previous log; only a fresh completed collection log increments
the actual count. sysmalloc reports unsupported GC checks separately.
